/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  edbatch02_constant_voltage  --  THE SAME RIG, one knob different, and the
  hand calculation failing for the opposite reason.

  Everything is identical to `edbatch01_constant_current` -- the same stack,
  the same two tanks, the same recirculation flow, the same xi, the same
  electrode term -- except that the rectifier is held at 4 V instead of
  3.5 A.  The difference between the two runs IS the difference between the
  two ways of driving a batch electrodialysis.

  WHAT THE RUN MEASURES (printed by this run; nothing here is a target):

      I                     4.365 A at t = 0  ->  2.018 A at t = 1500 s
      i/i_lim               0.3394  ->  0.4086     NEVER reaches 1
      demineralisation      61.59 %  ACTUAL
                            85.51 %  what the hand calculation predicts
      the hand calculation's own answer: the tank is EMPTY at t = 1754 s
      diluate              0.1 -> 0.03841 mol/kg
      kappa (diluate)      1.262 -> 0.4846 S/m
      E_mem (per pair)     0.0794 -> 0.1313 V
      specific energy       1.048 kWh per m3 of diluate treated
      material closure      machine level, as in edbatch01

  WHY THE CURRENT FALLS.  The stack equation is

      U = N ( E_mem + I R_pair ) + E_electrodes

  and BOTH terms inside the bracket move against the current as the run
  proceeds.  The diluate loses conductivity, so R_pair rises (0.0636 ->
  0.1119 Ohm per cell pair); and the concentration RATIO between the two
  tanks grows, so the Nernst back-EMF rises with it.  At fixed U the current
  has nowhere to go but down, and it more than halves.

  WHY IT NEVER CROSSES i_lim.  i_lim falls as the diluate depletes -- exactly
  as in edbatch01 -- but so does i, and here they fall together: the ratio
  ends at 0.409 having started at 0.339.  That is the reason a real bench rig
  is run this way when the membranes matter: constant voltage is
  self-limiting, and the price is time.  This case declares NO
  `overLimiting {}` model at all, and the run announces that default; it is
  irrelevant here precisely because the crossing never happens.

  THE LESSON, and it is the same lesson as edbatch01 arriving by a different
  road.  `demin_ideal` is Faraday's law at the INITIAL current -- a straight
  line, computed from this run's own I(0), own inventory and own xi.  It is
  24 percentage points optimistic at 1500 s, and not one of those points has
  anything to do with the limiting current: the current simply is not what it
  was.  Read `I_A` in trajectory.csv beside `deminActual` and `deminIdeal`
  and the whole of the gap is in that first column.

  WHAT IS PINNED AND WHAT IS NOT: as edbatch01.  The golden pins the answer
  this model gives; no measured batch electrodialysis is reproduced anywhere
  in this tree.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        rig;
        type        batchElectrodialysis;

        operation
        {
            stack          EUR2C-7P18;
            diluateFlow    175 L/h;

            //  CONSTANT VOLTAGE.  The current is solved from the stack
            //  equation at every instant and decays as the diluate loses
            //  conductivity and the Nernst back-EMF grows.
            voltage        4;             // V (raw SI)

            xi             0.9;
            E_electrodes   1.5;           // V (raw SI), lumped, announced

            //  No `overLimiting {}` block: the run announces the default and
            //  it never matters, because i/i_lim never reaches 1.
        }
    }
);
